= 2012
{scope}
{title2=2764}
{title2=B3: V}

Source paper: https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/AndreiStarinets/TUTORIALS_NOTES_GR/gr_cosmology_exam_paper_2012.pdf

The held cover dates this sitting to 13 June 2012. Lettered scopes index the successive separately marked source blocks; all four question options are answered. The <metric signature> is $(-,+,+,+)$. Wherever a cosmological density appears in a Friedmann equation below, $\rho$ denotes <mass density> or energy density divided by $c^2$; the corresponding physical <energy density> is $u=\rho c^2$.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Put $\mu=GM/c^2$, $A=1-2\mu/r$ and $B=1+2\mu/r$. For an affinely parametrized <geodesic>, use the unhalved quadratic <Lagrangian> $\mathcal L=-c^2A\dot t^2+B\dot{\mathbf r}^{,2}$. Its <Euler-Lagrange equations> are unchanged by this overall normalization. Since $t$ is a cyclic coordinate, $d(-2c^2A\dot t)/d\tau=0$, equivalently
$$
\boxed{\frac d{d\tau}(2c^2A\dot t)=0.}
$$
The spatial derivatives give $\partial\mathcal L/\partial\dot x^i=2B\dot x^i$ and $\partial\mathcal L/\partial x^i=-c^2\dot t^2A_i+\dot{\mathbf r}^{,2}B_i$, where $A_i=\partial_iA$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac d{d\tau}(2B\dot{\mathbf r})+c^2\dot t^2\nabla A-\dot{\mathbf r}^{,2}\nabla B=0.}
$$

For the <connection coefficients>, take $x^0=ct$, so $g_{00}=-A$ and $g_{ij}=B\delta_{ij}$. Substituting the diagonal <inverse metric> into the <Christoffel symbol> formula gives all nonzero component types, including lower-index symmetry:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\Gamma^0{}_{0i}=\Gamma^0{}_{i0}&=\frac{A_i}{2A}=\frac{\mu x_i}{Ar^3},\\
\Gamma^i{}_{00}&=\frac{A_i}{2B}=\frac{\mu x^i}{Br^3},\\
\Gamma^i{}_{jk}&=\frac{\delta^i_kB_j+\delta^i_jB_k-\delta_{jk}B_i}{2B}
=-\frac{\mu}{Br^3}(\delta^i_kx_j+\delta^i_jx_k-\delta_{jk}x^i).
\end{aligned}}
$$
All other component types vanish. If the time coordinate is $t$ rather than $ct$, replace $\Gamma^i{}_{00}$ by $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=c^2\Gamma^i{}_{00}$; the displayed time–space mixed coefficients are unchanged. Spatial indices in these formulas are raised with the background Euclidean metric.

For the circular orbit let $u=\mu/R$ and $\Omega=d\phi/dt=\omega/\dot t$. Both $A$ and $B$ are constant along it. The radial spatial equation gives $(1+u)\omega^2=(GM/R^3)\dot t^2$, so, treating the displayed metric coefficients algebraically, $\Omega^2=GM/[R^3(1+u)]$. The <weak-field approximation> securely determines the leading <Keplerian orbit> result
$$
\boxed{P_t\simeq2\pi\sqrt{\frac{R^3}{GM}},\qquad P_\tau=\frac{2\pi}{|\omega|},\qquad P_t=\dot t\,P_\tau.}
$$
The time normalization is $-c^2=-c^2A\dot t^2+BR^2\omega^2$, yielding $\dot t=1+3u/2+O(u^2)$ and $P_\tau/P_t=1-3u/2+O(u^2)$. One must distinguish the proper-time angular frequency in the supplied path from the coordinate-time angular frequency. The formal factor $1+u$ in the frequency equation comes from treating the truncated metric as exact; a complete relative post-Newtonian correction to the orbital period would also require the omitted higher-order temporal metric terms.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Use the <curvature sign convention>
$$
R^\rho{}_{\sigma\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\Gamma^\rho{}_{\nu\sigma}-\partial_\nu\Gamma^\rho{}_{\mu\sigma}+\Gamma^\rho{}_{\mu\lambda}\Gamma^\lambda{}_{\nu\sigma}-\Gamma^\rho{}_{\nu\lambda}\Gamma^\lambda{}_{\mu\sigma},
$$
which has the derivative ordering printed in the source for $R^i{}_{0j0}$. A <Christoffel symbol> is of order $\mu/r^2$, so its derivative is of order $\mu/r^3$, whereas a product of two connections is of order $\mu^2/r^4$. Their ratio is $\mu/r\ll1$. The metric is stationary, hence the temporal derivative term vanishes. Consequently
$$
R^i{}_{0j0}\simeq\partial_j\Gamma^i{}_{00}=-\frac12\partial_i\partial_jg_{00}.
$$
Here $g_{00}=-1+2\mu/r$, and $\partial_i\partial_j(1/r)=(3x_ix_j-r^2\delta_{ij})/r^5$. The <weak-field tidal curvature in a stationary metric> is
$$
\boxed{R^i{}_{0j0}=\frac{GM}{c^2r^3}\left(\delta_{ij}-3\frac{x^ix^j}{r^2}\right)+O(\mu^2/r^4).}
$$

**Printed sign inconsistency.** The source's positive half-Hessian and its final negative-prefactor expression are the opposites of this result. A concrete check at $(r,0,0)$ is $\Gamma^1{}_{00}\simeq\mu/r^2$, giving $\partial_1\Gamma^1{}_{00}=-2\mu/r^3$, while the printed final expression gives $+2\mu/r^3$. Thus both printed equalities cannot hold with its stated derivative ordering. Reversing the definition of the <Riemann curvature tensor> would give the printed final sign, but would also require reversing the curvature term in the <geodesic deviation> equation. The convention used here and in the next part gives the physical radial stretching and transverse compression.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

<Geodesic deviation> compares neighboring members of a family of freely falling trajectories. Subtracting their <geodesic equations> to first order in the connecting vector gives a covariant relative-acceleration equation governed by the <Riemann curvature tensor>. For a slowly moving satellite, $u^0=c\dot t\simeq c$ dominates the spatial components. Choose simultaneous spatial separation in a locally transported frame, take the <weak-field approximation>, and neglect terms quadratic in the small separation and higher velocity corrections. The leading spatial equation becomes
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^2\Delta^i}{d\tau^2}+c^2R^i{}_{0j0}\Delta^j\simeq0.}
$$
Replacing the transported covariant derivatives by ordinary derivatives is a leading local or Newtonian approximation; a rotating orbital frame would introduce additional inertial terms. In the convention established above, the instantaneous <tidal acceleration> stretches radial separations and compresses transverse ones.

The growth expected for nearby *different-radius* circular orbits is especially transparent from <Kepler's third law>. If the radii are $R$ and $R+\delta R$, their coordinate angular frequencies differ by $\delta\Omega\simeq-(3/2)\Omega\delta R/R$. Initially aligned orbits therefore acquire an along-track separation $R\delta\Omega\,t\simeq-(3/2)\Omega\delta R\,t$. Over the interval in which the nearby-orbit approximation remains valid, this produces a growing separation without needing to solve the deviation equation.

This growth is not universal or indefinitely unbounded. Equal-radius co-orbiting geodesics with a fixed phase offset have constant separation, and slightly inclined equal-radius circular orbits have bounded, periodically varying separation. Even different-radius circular orbits have bounded exact positions; their relative angle eventually wraps around. The secular drift statement assumes distinct radii and describes the local differential-orbit regime.

\Image[2012-nearby-circular-orbit-drift.png]
{title=Physical tidal stretching and the early differential drift of neighboring circular orbits}

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Take the unhalved geodesic <Lagrangian>
$$
\mathcal L=-c^2\alpha\dot t^2+\alpha^{-1}\dot r^2+r^2\dot\theta^2+r^2\sin^2\theta\dot\phi^2.
$$
The cyclic coordinates $t,\phi$ give $d(2c^2\alpha\dot t)/d\lambda=0$ and $d(2r^2\sin^2\theta\dot\phi)/d\lambda=0$. The other two <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
\frac d{d\lambda}(2r^2\dot\theta)-2r^2\sin\theta\cos\theta\dot\phi^2=0,
$$
$$
\frac d{d\lambda}\left(\frac{2\dot r}{\alpha}\right)+c^2\alpha'\dot t^2+\frac{\alpha'}{\alpha^2}\dot r^2-2r\dot\theta^2-2r\sin^2\theta\dot\phi^2=0.
$$
These reproduce the supplied geodesic equations. Expanding the derivatives and comparing with $\ddot x^\mu+\Gamma^\mu{}_{\nu\rho}\dot x^\nu\dot x^\rho=0$, in coordinates $(t,r,\theta,\phi)$, gives all nonzero <Christoffel symbols>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\Gamma^t{}_{tr}=\Gamma^t{}_{rt}&=\frac{\alpha'}{2\alpha},&
\Gamma^r{}_{tt}&=\frac{c^2\alpha\alpha'}2,&
\Gamma^r{}_{rr}&=-\frac{\alpha'}{2\alpha},\\
\Gamma^r{}_{\theta\theta}&=-r\alpha,&
\Gamma^r{}_{\phi\phi}&=-r\alpha\sin^2\theta,&
\Gamma^\theta{}_{r\theta}=\Gamma^\theta{}_{\theta r}&=\frac1r,\\
\Gamma^\phi{}_{r\phi}=\Gamma^\phi{}_{\phi r}&=\frac1r,&
\Gamma^\theta{}_{\phi\phi}&=-\sin\theta\cos\theta,&
\Gamma^\phi{}_{\theta\phi}=\Gamma^\phi{}_{\phi\theta}&=\cot\theta.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Here $\alpha'=-2r/r_S^2$. This is the <static patch of de Sitter spacetime>, with $0\leq r<r_S$ for the stationary observers used below. Apparent divergences at the polar axis, origin or horizon belong to the chosen coordinate chart; they are not themselves evidence of a curvature singularity.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For <radial null geodesics in a de Sitter static patch>, the conserved time quantity and the stated normalization at the origin give $\alpha\dot t=1$. The null condition then gives
$$
0=-c^2\alpha\dot t^2+\alpha^{-1}\dot r^2,\qquad \dot r^2=c^2\alpha^2\dot t^2=c^2.
$$
The emitted outgoing ray selects the positive branch, so
$$
\boxed{\dot r=c,\qquad\dot t=\frac1{1-r^2/r_S^2}.}
$$
The ingoing branch would have $\dot r=-c$ instead. Set the affine parameter to zero at emission. Then $r=c\lambda$, and integrating $dt/dr=1/[c(1-r^2/r_S^2)]$ gives
$$
t=\frac{r_S}{c}\operatorname{artanh}\frac r{r_S},\qquad \boxed{r(t)=r_S\tanh\frac{ct}{r_S}.}
$$
The coordinate time diverges as $r\to r_S$, although the outgoing affine parameter reaches $r_S/c$ there. This is a coordinate-horizon effect: a local stationary observer inside the patch measures radial distance $dr/\sqrt\alpha$ and proper time $\sqrt\alpha\,dt$, whose ratio along the ray is still $c$.

\Image[2012-de-sitter-null-rays-and-clock-redshift.png]
{title=Radial light rays in static coordinates and the redshift of a stationary emitter}

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

For the <static-emitter redshift in a de Sitter patch>, a stationary clock at radius $r_E$ satisfies $d\tau_E=\sqrt{\alpha_E}\,dt_E$. The geometry is stationary, so successive inward pulses emitted at the same radius have the same coordinate travel delay. Their coordinate arrival interval is therefore $\Delta t_O=\Delta t_E$. At the central receiver $\alpha_O=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{\Delta\tau_O=\frac{\Delta\tau_E}{\sqrt{1-r_E^2/r_S^2}}.}
$$
For $r_E=r_S/2$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{\Delta\tau_O=\frac2{\sqrt3}\Delta\tau_E,\qquad 1+z=\frac{\nu_E}{\nu_O}=\frac2{\sqrt3},\qquad z=\frac2{\sqrt3}-1.}
$$
The pulses are redshifted at the centre. At fixed proper emission period, taking a stationary emitter toward the horizon gives $\Delta\tau_O\to\infty$, $\nu_O\to0$ and $z\to\infty$. The inward coordinate travel delay also diverges. A stationary worldline at the horizon itself is not timelike, and the proper acceleration required to hold an emitter static diverges in this limit; the result concerns a sequence of idealized emitters with $r_E<r_S$, not a stationary physical lighthouse exactly on the horizon.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Normalize $a(t_0)=1$, taking $a$ dimensionless and $r$ as a length, so the source's curvature constant $k$ has units of inverse length squared. With densities in the mass-equivalent convention, the first <Friedmann equation> is
$$
\boxed{H^2=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\sum_i\rho_i-\frac{kc^2}{a^2}+\frac{\Lambda c^2}{3}.}
$$
The sum can include dust, radiation and other material components; vacuum energy must be represented either in that sum or by $\Lambda$, without double counting. For physical energy densities $u_i$, replace $\rho_i$ by $u_i/c^2$. The companion acceleration equation is $\ddot a/a=-(4\pi G/3)\sum_i(\rho_i+3p_i/c^2)+\Lambda c^2/3$, with energy conservation $\dot\rho_i+3H(\rho_i+p_i/c^2)=0$ for separately conserved components.

For an empty universe with $\Lambda=0$, $H^2=-kc^2/a^2$. On the expanding branch with $H_0>0$,
$$
\boxed{k=-\frac{H_0^2}{c^2}<0.}
$$
Equivalently, in the convention where the normalized curvature label is $k=-1$ and the scale factor has length units, $a_0=c/H_0$. A rescaling of comoving coordinates changes the numerical magnitude of $k$ but not its negative sign. The degenerate empty case $H_0=0$ has $k=0$ and a constant scale factor; it does not have a finite age $1/H_0$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For the expanding empty model, $\dot a=H_0$ in the preceding normalization. If $t_B$ denotes the origin of the expanding slicing,
$$
\boxed{a(t)=H_0(t-t_B),\qquad q_0=-\frac{a\ddot a}{\dot a^2}=0,\qquad t_0-t_B=H_0^{-1}.}
$$
The constant-time spatial slices are negatively curved <hyperbolic spaces>, with curvature radius $R_{\rm curv}=a/\sqrt{-k}=c(t-t_B)$ and three-dimensional scalar curvature $6k/a^2$. In the usual simply connected description they are copies of hyperbolic three-space. This is the <Milne model>: its four-dimensional spacetime is actually flat. Setting $T=(t-t_B)\cosh\chi$ and $R=c(t-t_B)\sinh\chi$ converts the metric to the interior of a future light cone in <Minkowski spacetime>. Its zero-scale-factor boundary is a slicing boundary, not a curvature singularity.

Add separately conserved <pressureless matter>, with present mass-equivalent density $\rho_{M0}$. Define the <cosmological density parameters>
$$
\Omega_M=\frac{8\pi G\rho_{M0}}{3H_0^2},\qquad \Omega_K=-\frac{kc^2}{H_0^2},\qquad\Omega_M+\Omega_K=1.
$$
Then
$$
\boxed{H^2=H_0^2(\Omega_Ma^{-3}+\Omega_Ka^{-2}),\qquad q(a)=\frac{\Omega_M}{2(\Omega_M+\Omega_Ka)},\qquad q_0=\frac{\Omega_M}{2}.}
$$
Curvature and dust have equal magnitudes at $a_K=\Omega_M/\Omega_K$, giving
$$
\boxed{1+z_K=\frac{\Omega_K}{\Omega_M},\qquad z_K=\frac{\Omega_K}{\Omega_M}-1.}
$$
For the almost-empty open case $0<\Omega_M<1/2$, curvature domination begins at positive redshift. Equality is today for $\Omega_M=1/2$ and in the future for $1/2<\Omega_M<1$. With $\Omega_M=0$, curvature dominates at all times and there is no finite matter–curvature equality; with $\Omega_K=0$, curvature never dominates.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

A radial null ray satisfies $dr/\sqrt{1-kr^2}=\pm c\,dt/a(t)$. Define the present-normalized radial <comoving distance>
$$
D_C=\int_{t_E}^{t_0}\frac{c\,dt}{a(t)}=\int_0^{r_E}\frac{dr}{\sqrt{1-kr^2}}.
$$
For $k<0$, write $\kappa=\sqrt{-k}=H_0\sqrt{\Omega_K}/c$. Integration gives $D_C=\operatorname{arsinh}(\kappa r_E)/\kappa$, so $r_E=\sinh(\kappa D_C)/\kappa$. Two neighboring pulses on the same comoving path obey $\Delta t_0/a_0=\Delta t_E/a_E$, hence $1+z=1/a_E$. For the <angular diameter distance in an open dust universe>, a transverse source size $\ell=a_Er_E\,\delta\theta$ defines the <angular diameter distance> $D_A=\ell/\delta\theta$. Thus
$$
\boxed{D_A(z)=\frac1{1+z}\frac{c}{H_0\sqrt{\Omega_K}}\sinh\left(\frac{H_0\sqrt{\Omega_K}}cD_C(z)\right).}
$$

Since $dt=da/(aH)$, the dust-plus-curvature expansion gives
$$
\boxed{D_C(z)=\frac c{H_0}\int_{1/(1+z)}^1\frac{da}{\sqrt a\sqrt{\Omega_M+(1-\Omega_M)a}}.}
$$
At a specified source redshift, the only independent cosmological parameters in this integral are $\Omega_M$ and $H_0$. The distance necessarily also has a source-position argument; it cannot be a constant for all sources.

For $0<\Omega_M<1$, the integral can be written
$$
D_C=\frac{2c}{H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_M}}\left[\operatorname{arsinh}\sqrt{\frac{1-\Omega_M}{\Omega_M}}-\operatorname{arsinh}\sqrt{\frac{1-\Omega_M}{\Omega_M(1+z)}}\right].
$$
The apparently singular parameter endpoints have regular limits: $\Omega_M=0$ gives $D_C=(c/H_0)\ln(1+z)$, and $\Omega_M=1$ gives $D_C=(2c/H_0)[1-(1+z)^{-1/2}]$. The flat-curvature distance limit is $D_A=D_C/(1+z)$. For a negative $\Omega_K$, the transverse distance uses sine with $\sqrt{-\Omega_K}$ instead of hyperbolic sine; that closed case is outside the almost-empty open model, but explains the convention behind the square root.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

At fixed radial <comoving distance> and fixed emission scale factor, open spatial geometry has transverse radius $\sinh(\kappa D_C)/\kappa>D_C$. Hence its <angular diameter distance> exceeds the flat value, and a standard object of fixed proper size has
$$
\boxed{\delta\theta=\ell/D_A\quad\text{smaller in the open geometry}.}
$$
The same comparison can be expressed at fixed proper radial distance on the emission slice. A comparison at fixed *redshift* must additionally account for the different expansion histories in $D_C(z)$; curvature and expansion effects must not be silently conflated.

Known-size <cosmological standard rulers> with measured redshifts provide $D_A(z)$ through their angular sizes. The function depends on the spatial-curvature parameter directly and on matter, radiation or dark energy through the expansion history. For a comoving ruler such as a calibrated <baryon acoustic oscillation> scale, the transverse angular measurement determines $(1+z)D_A$. Angular acoustic scales in the <cosmic microwave background> similarly probe the ratio of a calibrated early-time length to a cosmological distance. A range of redshifts constrains parameter-dependent shapes and the overall distance scale, but one angular measurement cannot uniquely separate all density parameters. Ruler calibration, source evolution and lensing must be modeled, and independent expansion or distance information helps break the degeneracies.

\Image[2012-open-universe-angular-distances.png]
{title=Transverse curvature geometry and angular diameter distances in dust-plus-curvature models}

== 4
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

For a separately conserved homogeneous component, energy conservation gives $d(\rho c^2a^3)=-p\,d(a^3)$, equivalently $\dot\rho+3H(\rho+p/c^2)=0$. Dust has negligible pressure, while isotropic radiation has $p_\gamma=\rho_\gamma c^2/3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\rho_M=\rho_{M0}a^{-3},\qquad\rho_\gamma=\rho_{\gamma0}a^{-4}.}
$$
The matter factor is volume dilution; photons additionally lose energy by <cosmological redshift>. Nonrelativistic baryons and <cold dark matter> both contribute to the matter term, so $\Omega_M=\Omega_B+\Omega_C$. At equality $\rho_{M0}a_{\rm eq}^{-3}=\rho_{\gamma0}a_{\rm eq}^{-4}$, giving
$$
\boxed{a_{\rm eq}=\frac{\Omega_\gamma}{\Omega_B+\Omega_C},\qquad1+z_{\rm eq}=\frac{\Omega_B+\Omega_C}{\Omega_\gamma}.}
$$
This is equality for the source's matter-plus-photon model. Additional relativistic species would contribute to total radiation and shift the equality value. Although the source calls the $\rho$'s energy densities, its Friedmann equation and later $\rho_\gamma c^2$ show that their consistent SI meaning is mass-equivalent density.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For thermal photons, the available thermal length is $\ell_T=\hbar c/(k_BT)$. The number of occupied modes per volume scales as $\ell_T^{-3}$, while their characteristic energy is of order $k_BT=\hbar c/\ell_T$. Thus $n_\gamma\propto\ell_T^{-3}$ and $u_\gamma=\rho_\gamma c^2\propto\hbar c\,\ell_T^{-4}$. More explicitly, the two <photon polarizations> and the <Bose-Einstein distribution> give
$$
n_\gamma=\frac{2\zeta(3)}{\pi^2}\left(\frac{k_BT}{\hbar c}\right)^3,\qquad u_\gamma=\frac{\pi^2}{15}\hbar c\left(\frac{k_BT}{\hbar c}\right)^4,
$$
consistent with the rounded coefficients supplied in the source.

Use its definition $s=n_\gamma/n_B$, with $n_B=\rho_B/m_p=\Omega_B\rho_c/m_p$ and $\rho_c$ the <mass-equivalent cosmological critical density>. Writing $Q=k_BT/(\hbar c)$, the photon energy formula gives $Q^4=\Omega_\gamma\rho_cc/(0.66\hbar)$. Therefore
$$
s=\frac{0.24m_p}{\Omega_B\rho_c}\left(\frac{\Omega_\gamma\rho_cc}{0.66\hbar}\right)^{3/4}
=\boxed{\frac{0.24}{0.66^{3/4}}\left(\frac{m_p^4c^3}{\rho_c\hbar^3}\right)^{1/4}\frac{\Omega_\gamma^{3/4}}{\Omega_B}}.
$$
This <photon-to-baryon ratio from density parameters> has leading coefficient $0.328\simeq0.33$, as required. For an illustrative estimate, take the stated $\rho_c=10^{-26}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\Omega_\gamma=5\times10^{-5}$ and $\Omega_B=0.05$. Then $\boxed{s\sim1.4\times10^9}$, i.e. of order a billion photons per baryon. The source alone supplies no numerical density parameters, so an estimate must state such additional inputs.

Strictly, this $s$ is the <photon-to-baryon ratio>, rather than the thermodynamic entropy per baryon itself. For a thermal photon gas, $S_\gamma/(k_BN_B)=[4u_\gamma/(3k_BTn_\gamma)]s\simeq3.6s$ with the exact blackbody coefficients. The ratio used in the source is proportional to the radiation entropy per baryon under adiabatic evolution.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Assume an ideal nondegenerate hydrogen gas in <thermal equilibrium> and <chemical equilibrium>, with ground-state atoms and charge neutrality. Let $n_p=n_e=Xn_B$ and $n_H=(1-X)n_B$. The ground-state <Saha equation>, with the usual electron/proton spin factors cancelling the atomic ground-state degeneracy, is
$$
\frac{n_en_p}{n_H}=\left(\frac{m_ek_BT}{2\pi\hbar^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{-B/(k_BT)}.
$$
Invert it and use $n_B=n_\gamma/s=(0.24/s)(k_BT/(\hbar c))^3$. This gives
$$
\frac{1-X}{X^2}=\frac{0.24(2\pi)^{3/2}}s\left(\frac{k_BT}{m_ec^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{B/(k_BT)},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\frac{1-X}{X^2}\simeq\frac{3.8}s\left(\frac{k_BT}{m_ec^2}\right)^{3/2}\exp\frac{B}{k_BT}.}
$$
Here $m_e$ is the electron mass denoted $M_e$ in the source, and $0.24(2\pi)^{3/2}=3.78$. At $X=1/2$, the left side is two. At $k_BT$ of order $B$, the right side is extremely small because $1/s\sim10^{-9}$ and $B/(m_ec^2)\ll1$. A large exponential is needed to compensate, requiring $B/(k_BT)\gg1$ rather than a temperature of order the binding energy.

Physically, there are enormously many photons per baryon, so even a small high-energy tail of the thermal photon distribution can maintain ionization. Recombination is delayed until that tail is sufficiently depleted. This explains the low recombination temperature without solving the equilibrium equation; actual cosmological recombination also eventually requires rate equations once equilibrium fails.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

<Big Bang nucleosynthesis> is a kinetic process in an expanding universe. A common particle temperature does not imply chemical equilibrium for every nuclear reaction: reaction rates must also exceed the expansion rate and connect the required species efficiently. Around $10^9\,\mathrm K$, nuclear abundances retain the history of earlier freeze-out and cannot be inferred from equilibrium Boltzmann factors alone.

In particular, weak reactions cease to maintain <neutron-proton chemical equilibrium>. Subsequent <free-neutron decay after freeze-out> changes the surviving neutron inventory. The large photon-to-baryon ratio delays deuterium survival through photodissociation; once the <deuterium bottleneck> opens, reactions rapidly bind many surviving neutrons into helium-4. Coulomb barriers, decreasing density and further freeze-out prevent unrestricted equilibration of all nuclei. The final helium abundance therefore requires a reaction network and expansion history, not just the temperature and nuclear binding energies.

For illustration, if essentially all available neutrons end in helium-4 and the <neutron-to-proton ratio> at that stage is $r$, simple particle counting gives $Y_{\rm He}\simeq2r/(1+r)$. The crucial input $r$ is itself set by out-of-equilibrium history. Kinetic thermalization of the plasma can remain effective while these composition-changing processes are out of equilibrium.
